
             Rough documentation for TechScan 1.0

                             LEGAL

   TechScan is FreeWare. It may be distributed in unmodified
form for FREE; no charges may be taken for distribution except
nominal copying fees. Although it's free, all the files are
still copyrighted by Lauri Aalto. Permission is hereby granted
to include TechScan in public domain software collections,
upload it to bulletin board systems and ftp archives.

                             WHAT

   TechScan is a simple, quick and dirty tool written in
assembler (took 5 minutes) for all TechnoBBS sysops who have
files online. It rescans your file tree and updates the global
list of files thus making those files not added with
TechCon-aware utilities listable and downloadable. It's exactly
the same as the update filelist entry in the special menu of
TechnoBBS. It just doesn't need a node running and it can be
run from scripts, that's the difference.

                         INSTALLATION

   Simple. Just move the executable to somewhere on your
command search path (BBS:Bin preferred) and it's done. You're a
sysop, you should be able to do this without advice.

                             USAGE

   Just run it. TechScan takes no arguments, all it does is
that it sends a message to TechCon telling it to update the
global list of files.
   TechScan is pure. It can be run from Workbench also, but
there will be no output. Icons included (without permission)
are standard MagicWb icons copyrighted by Martin Huttenloher.
If they look ugly, you probably aren't using the MagicWb
screenmode and palette.

                      MORE ADVANCED USAGE

   Having TechScan executed regulary may be a good idea. One
way to do this is to add it to your User-Startup file or drag
it's icon to your WbStartup drawer. I personally have it in my
crontab. A typical crotab entry would look something like this:

# Rescan the file base every night at 0500 hours.

0 5 * * * TechScan

                             NOTE

   As rescanning and sorting is very CPU consuming, it's
adviced that you run TechScan on non-rush hours as it locks up
TechCon and tasks running at lower priorities for a while
depending on the amount of files and speed of your HD/CPU.

                    BUGS & FUTURE VERSIONS

   As TechScan is as simple as it is, there should be no bugs.
If you for some reason discover any, don't hesitate to tell me
so I can fix them. For now I see no reason to create any future
versions, but if you do, tell me, please!

